ssurance Committee","date":"29/06/2026","key":true},{"title":"Draft 2025 26 Annual Governance Statement","url":"http://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk/documents/s174275/draft-2025-26-annual-governance-statement.pdf","committee":"Governance & Assurance Committee","date":"29/06/2026","key":true},{"title":"APPENDIX 1 - Annual Governance and Assurance Committee","url":"http://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk/documents/s174279/appendix-1-annual-governance-and-assurance-committee.pdf","committee":"Governance & Assurance Committee","date":"29/06/2026","key":true},{"title":"Appendix B 2025-26 Redbridge Pension Fund - Audit Planning Report","url":"http://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk/documents/s174286/appendix-b-2025-26-redbridge-pension-fund-audit-planning-rep.pdf","committee":"Governance & Assurance Committee","date":"29/06/2026","key":true}],"read_documents":[],"calendar":{"upcoming":[{"date":"2026-09-23","committee":"Pension Fund Committee","time":"10.30 a.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Council","time":"7.00 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-12","committee":"Local Pension Board","time":"5.30 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-19","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.00 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-22","committee":"Cabinet","time":"7.00 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-02","committee":"Governance & Assurance Committee","time":"7.00 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-03","committee":"Children and Education Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.00 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-09","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.00 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-16","committee":"Cabinet","time":"7.00 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-07","committee":"Governance & Assurance Committee","time":"7.00 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-14","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.00 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Health and Care Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.00 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Cabinet","time":"7.00 p.m.","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Redbridge's budget gap widens as council struggles to deliver planned savings","lead":"Redbridge Council is forecasting a \u00a38.485m overspend for 2026/27, nearly \u00a33m worse than expected in summer. The shortfall has grown despite the council relying on \u00a316.117m of mitigating actions to reach even this position. The Cabinet heard that only 17 per cent of the council's \u00a315.838m annual savings programme is on track; 55 per cent is at risk of non-delivery. The High Needs Block, which funds support for children with complex educational needs, is facing a separate \u00a317.352m overspend, building a cumulative schools-funding deficit of \u00a326.487m by year end. The council remains dependent on exceptional financial support from government while it rebuilds its finances, with no headroom to absorb further budget pressures without dipping into reserves.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"Budgetary Control Report for Quarter 1 (2) - Report (1)","paper_url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk/documents/s175109/175109.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Redbridge forecasts \u00a38.5m budget overspend as savings plans falter and school funding crisis deepens","lead":"Redbridge Council is heading for a \u00a38.485m overspend in its day-to-day spending this year, the Cabinet heard on 17 September, a deterioration of \u00a33.061m in just one month. The council is relying on \u00a316.117m of emergency mitigating actions to reach even this position. The underlying picture is weaker: \u00a34.243m of the overspend is permanent and will carry forward to next year, making budget-setting in 2027\u201328 \"far more difficult\", the council warns. The council's savings programme is faltering badly. Of \u00a315.838m in planned cuts and efficiencies, only 17 per cent is on track; 55.6 per cent is at risk of non-delivery. Two areas are in crisis: temporary accommodation is overspending by \u00a33.490m because promised savings have not materialised, and the high-needs school funding block, which supports children with special educational needs, is forecast to overspend by \u00a317.352m, building a cumulative deficit of \u00a326.487m by the year's end. Redbridge remains dependent on exceptional financial support, a government permission to borrow to cover day-to-day spending.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"Budgetary Control Report for Quarter 1 (2) - Report (1)","paper_url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk/documents/s175109/175109.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Redbridge overspend surges to \u00a38.5m in first quarter as savings plan falters","lead":"Redbridge Council's forecast spending gap has widened sharply to \u00a38.485m in the first quarter of 2026-27, up \u00a33.061m in a single month from the previous month's position. The deterioration reveals a council struggling to deliver the savings required to balance its budget. Only 17 per cent of planned savings of \u00a315.838m are on track; 55.6 per cent are classified as at risk. Most concerning, \u00a34.243m of the overspend is ongoing and will carry forward to next year, the Budget Control Report warns, making the 2027-28 budget-setting \"far more difficult\". The cabinet was told the council has \"limited scope to absorb further budget pressures without impacting the level of reserves\". Redbridge remains dependent on exceptional financial support, the government mechanism for councils in financial difficulty, while attempting to rebuild stability.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"Budgetary Control Report for Quarter 1 (2) - Report (1)","paper_url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk/documents/s175109/175109.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Appendix B - Borrowing as at 31 March 26","paper_url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk/documents/s175191/175191.pdf","headline":"Portfolio shifting from PWLB-dominated (85%) to market-dependent (100%) as \u00a3479m PWLB is fully repaid without replacement.","detail":"Opening balance shows \u00a3479.171m PWLB (85% of \u00a3564.171m total) and \u00a385m market/bond (15%). Repayment schedules show \u00a3504.171m PWLB being fully amortized while \u00a3589.171m market/bond is managed through refinancing. Council is transitioning to full market financing.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Shift from fixed-rate PWLB to market-rate debt changes interest rate exposure; market-dependent financing carries greater refinancing and pricing risk than PWLB access.","figures":"Opening PWLB: \u00a3479,171,000; Opening market/bond: \u00a385,000,000; PWLB total repayment: \u00a3504,171,000; Market/bond total repayment: \u00a3589,171,000","quote":"Bal c/f  479,171,000  85,000,000  -  -   -","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Appendix B - Borrowing as at 31 March 26","paper_url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk/documents/s175191/175191.pdf","headline":"\u00a375m refinancing required in 2069/70 represents 12.73% of total portfolio and the largest future debt event 43 years out.","detail":"Market/bond debt repayments are scheduled through 2069/70. The final year shows \u00a375m of borrowing due, the single largest refinancing event in the projection. Portfolio maturity extends 43 years from the report date, creating long-dated refinancing obligations.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Refinancing needs four decades ahead create sustained interest rate risk and refinancing risk; the council must maintain capital markets access and investor confidence over an unusually extended period.","figures":"\u00a375,000,000 scheduled for 2069/70; represents 12.73% of total portfolio; portfolio matures across 43-year period","quote":"2069/70 - - - 75,000,000 75,000,000 12.73","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Appendix B - Borrowing as at 31 March 26","paper_url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk/documents/s175191/175191.pdf","headline":"PWLB borrowing ends after 2026/27; no further PWLB access planned despite \u00a3479m existing PWLB stock.","detail":"The council is taking \u00a325m PWLB borrowing in 2026/27, after which the PWLB borrow column shows '-' (zero) for all subsequent years through 2069/70. The entire PWLB portfolio of \u00a3504.171m total will be repaid with no replacement from PWLB sources. This signals either PWLB capacity exhaustion (potentially due to Exceptional Finance Provision restrictions) or a deliberate shift to market financing.","category":"borrowing","matters":"PWLB is typically cheaper than market funding; loss of access increases future borrowing costs and signals the council is now dependent on capital markets for any refinancing or new borrowing.","figures":"\u00a325,000,000 PWLB borrowing in 2026/27 only; \u00a3504,171,000 total PWLB principal (\u00a3479,171,000 opening plus \u00a325,000,000 new); zero PWLB borrowing from 2027/28 onward","quote":"2026/27  25,000,000  63,200,000  -  -  63,200,000  10.73","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Treasury Management Outturn 25-26 for Council - Report Final","paper_url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk/documents/s175189/175189.pdf","headline":"Investment balances fell by \u00a327m to \u00a3116.800m as council deployed cash for capital funding","detail":"The council's available-for-investment cash balances decreased from \u00a3143.800m to \u00a3116.800m over the year. Per the council's explanation, this was partly due to timing differences in cash flows but primarily due to temporary deployment of internal resources to fund the capital programme, consistent with the deliberate under-borrowing strategy.","category":"reserves","matters":"Depletion of liquid reserves indicates the council is consuming its cash buffer to avoid external borrowing. While deliberate, this reduces flexibility and liquidity headroom for unforeseen demands.","figures":"Investment balances \u00a3116.800m (31/03/26) vs \u00a3143.800m (31/03/25), decrease of \u00a327m (19% reduction)","quote":"The overall level of cash balances decreased by \u00a327m, this was due in part to timing differences between the receipt and payment of cash transactions, and the temporary use of internal resources to fund the capital programme.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Council","paper_title":"Treasury Management Outturn 25-26 for Council - Report Final","paper_url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk/documents/s175189/175189.pdf","headline":"No PWLB debt rescheduling opportunities identified despite annual portfolio review; refinancing windows closed","detail":"The council kept its borrowing portfolio under review throughout 2025/26 and considered debt rescheduling opportunities, but determined that no cost-beneficial options existed. The interest rate differential between current loan rates and replacement rates was insufficient to justify refinancing action. This indicates the PWLB rate structure is not moving favourably for the council.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Absence of refinancing opportunities indicates interest rates are not moving in the council's favour; the council remains locked into its current debt stack and cannot improve its cost position through market activity.","figures":"Zero PWLB loans rescheduled or prematurely repaid in 2025/26","quote":"No PWLB loans were rescheduled or prematurely repaid. This was because the PWLB interest rate differential between the actual loan rate and repayment loan rate would not have provided a cost benefit to the Council.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-17"},"name":"Redbridge","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-19","since_iso":"2026-08-20","distress":true,"headline":"HRA housing programme accelerated to \u00a358.6m in 2026/27, up from originally planned \u00a322.5m, funded from future years' approved provisions.","lead":"A \u00a336.5m acceleration (63% increase to planned spend) materially changes the council's borrowing need and cash flow profile for 2026/27. This can require either accelerated drawdown of existing facilities or new borrowing headroom. The stated reasons\u2026","also":["23 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee on 2026-10-19","n_findings":24,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Appendix B Capital Q1- 202627 (1)","top_paper_url":"https://moderngov.redbridge.gov.uk/documents/s175111/175111.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":92,"news_drafts_total":3}}